{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01E4RA2JUEN5dxDCGrpY8jdX","type":"tool_result","content":"882\truntime environment\" [source: k8s-docs-components-2026-08-23]. Chapter 3 flags the verb:\n883\tthe node's collective job is to **maintain** running Pods, \"not to start them once and\n884\tstand down.\"\n885\t\n886\t## The three\n887\t\n888\t**kubelet** — \"An agent that runs on each node in the cluster. It makes sure that\n889\tcontainers are running in a Pod. The kubelet takes a set of PodSpecs that are provided\n890\tthrough various mechanisms and ensures that the containers described in those PodSpecs are\n891\trunning and healthy. **The kubelet doesn't manage containers which were not created by\n892\tKubernetes.**\"\n893\t\n894\t**kube-proxy (optional)** — \"a network proxy that runs on each node in your cluster,\n895\timplementing **part** of the Kubernetes Service concept. kube-proxy maintains network rules\n896\ton nodes.\" Optional \"if you use a network plugin that implements packet forwarding for\n897\tServices by itself, and providing equivalent behavior to kube-proxy.\"\n898\t\n899\t**container runtime** — \"A fundamental component that empowers Kubernetes to run containers\n900\teffectively. It is responsible for managing the execution and lifecycle of containers\n901\twithin the Kubernetes environment.\" containerd, CRI-O, or any CRI implementation.\n902\t\n903\t## The clause with practical teeth\n904\t\n905\t\"If you SSH to a node and start a container by hand with the container runtime directly,\n906\tthe kubelet does not manage it. It won't restart it, won't maintain it, won't clean it up.\"\n907\tChapter 3 draws the two-way consequence: it is why hand-started containers don't appear\n908\tthrough `kubectl`, **and** why node-level debugging needs a node-level tool (`crictl`,\n909\tCh 13 §5 — a pointer Chapter 2 already made identically at ch02:602).\n910\t\n911\t## What §3 adds beyond Chapter 2: position\n912\t\n913\tThe container runtime \"is the one node component that is not Kubernetes software at all.\n914\tIt's a separate project, swappable, and not authored by the Kubernetes project: Kubernetes\n915\tdefines the interface and accepts any implementation of it.\"\n916\t\n917\t## ⚑ RULE 6 — this section broadens Chapter 2's named pattern\n918\t\n919\tChapter 3 §3 continues: *\"That is the same architectural instinct you saw with etcd. Where\n920\ta good general-purpose component already exists, Kubernetes defines an interface and uses\n921\tit rather than reimplementing it.\"* **Two problems.**\n922\t\n923\t**(a) etcd is not in the enumeration.** Chapter 2's pattern is *\"Kubernetes defines an\n924\tinterface and lets the **ecosystem** implement it\"* (ch02:598), and its sourced list is\n925\t**CRI, CSI, CNI, device plugins, and API extensions**\n926\t[source: k8s-docs-extending-kubernetes-2026-08-23]. Kubernetes does not define an etcd\n927\tinterface; it consumes a general-purpose datastore. True of the runtime, true-but-different\n928\tof etcd — calling them \"the same\" broadens a sourced list past what it says.\n929\t\n930\t**(b) The name is dropped.** ch02:596–598 is a section titled \"The pattern to name now\"\n931\twhose ⚓ tells the reader *\"Give this move a name in your head, because you are about to see\n932\tit three more times.\"* Chapter 3 hits the **first recurrence**, re-derives it in fresh\n933\twords, and never uses the name or bears back. That is a missed spaced-retrieval event on a\n934\ttheme B3 tracks and Ch 17's secondary Zenith depends on.\n935\t\n936\t**Fix:** keep the named pattern for the container runtime, cross-beared to Ch 2 §4;\n937\tdescribe etcd as the adjacent-but-distinct instinct of reusing an existing general-purpose\n938\tcomponent.\n939\t\n940\t## ★ Fixed Point — the full census (verbatim)\n941\t\n942\t> **Control plane:** kube-apiserver, etcd, kube-scheduler, kube-controller-manager, and\n943\t> cloud-controller-manager *(optional — absent on premises and on your laptop)*.\n944\t>\n945\t> **Node** (on every node): kubelet, kube-proxy *(optional — unnecessary if a network\n946\t> plugin provides equivalent packet forwarding)*, and the container runtime.\n947\t>\n948\t> Eight names. Two of them optional, for two different reasons. One of them — the\n949\t> container runtime — is not Kubernetes software.\n950\t\n951\t## Related\n952\t\n953\t[[control-plane-components]] · [[optional-components]] · [[api-server-hub]]\n954\t=== END WRITE ===\n955\t\n956\t=== WRITE C:\\dev\\lodestar\\certcomp\\..\\Book-KCNA/knowledge-base/concepts/api-server-hub.md ===\n957\t# Concept: The API server as hub — \"watching, not telling\"\n958\t\n959\t**Home:** Chapter 3 §5 · **Competency:** D1.1 · **Status:** canonical\n960\t**Chapter 3's second-most-important idea**, after the control loop.\n961\t\n962\t## The four facts that compose into a shape\n963\t\n964\t1. \"The API server is the front end for the Kubernetes control plane.\"\n965\t   [source: k8s-docs-cluster-architecture-2026-08-23]\n966\t2. etcd is \"the backing store for all cluster data.\" [same]\n967\t3. \"Kubernetes has a hub-and-spoke API pattern. All API usage from nodes and the Pods they\n968\t   run terminates at the API server, and none of the other control-plane components are\n969\t   designed to expose remote services.\"\n970\t   [source: k8s-docs-control-plane-node-communication-2026-08-24 — ⚑ NOT ON DISK]\n971\t4. A controller \"might carry the action out itself; more commonly, in Kubernetes, a\n972\t   controller will send messages to the API server that have useful side effects.\"\n973\t   [source: k8s-docs-controllers-2026-08-23]\n974\t\n975\tChapter 3's image for the result: **\"Not a chain of command. A chart table: one surface\n976\teveryone works from, and no orders issued across it.\"**\n977\t\n978\t## ⚓ The reusable sentence (verbatim)\n979\t\n980\t> **The coordination mechanism in Kubernetes is watching, not telling.** Components don't\n981\t> send each other instructions. They observe shared state and act on what they see.\n982\t\n983\t## Two precisions the chapter is careful about — preserve both\n984\t\n985\t**1. etcd confinement is a RECOMMENDATION, not an invariant.** \"Access to etcd is\n986\tequivalent to root permission in the cluster, so **ideally** only the API server should\n987\thave access to it\" [source: k8s-docs-etcd-access-control-2026-08-24 — ⚑ NOT ON DISK].\n988\tChapter 3 states this explicitly: \"Read that as what it is: a strong recommendation with a\n989\tsecurity reason behind it, not a law of physics.\" **Do not harden it downstream.**\n990\t\n991\t**2. The hub describes STATE movement, not every connection.** \"The API server does open\n992\tconnections to kubelets: that is how fetching Pod logs, attaching to a running container,\n993\tand port-forwarding work. Those paths carry a session, not an instruction. Nothing\n994\ttravelling on them tells a kubelet what ought to be running.\"\n995\t\n996\tAn earlier draft asserted the absolute form; a prior AUTHOR-REVIEW records that it was\n997\tsoftened deliberately after the source was fetched. **Do not restore the absolute\n998\tphrasing.**\n999\t\n1000\t## The submission story (component altitude)\n1001\t\n1002\t1. Request arrives at the API server; validated and persisted. **Nothing is running yet.**\n1003\t2. The description is now part of cluster state, visible to anything watching.\n1004\t3. The scheduler notices a Pod with no node, selects one, records the selection back\n1005\t   through the API server.\n1006\t4. The kubelet on that node notices and starts the containers through the runtime.\n1007\t5. Status flows back the same way.\n1008\t\n1009\t\"Read the verbs. *Notices. Selects. Records. Notices.* At no point does one component\n1010\tinstruct another.\"\n1011\t\n1012\t## Figure\n1013\t\n1014\t`ch03-fig04-request-path-through-apiserver`. The caption instructs the reader to look for\n1015\tthe arrow that **isn't** drawn: there is none between any two of the surrounding\n1016\tcomponents. The figure is scoped to the **state/API path** and deliberately does not draw\n1017\tthe outbound logs/attach/port-forward paths.\n1018\t\n1019\t## ⚑ Forward-bearing split needed\n1020\t\n1021\tCh 3 §5 bears the outbound paths to \"Ch 13 — the debugging commands that ride those\n1022\toutbound paths.\" B2 gives `logs --previous` to Ch 13 but **`exec` and `port-forward` to\n1023\tCh 16.** Split the bearing.\n1024\t\n1025\t## Downstream obligations\n1026\t\n1027\t| Chapter | Obligation |\n1028\t|---|---|\n1029\t| Ch 8 | The auth → authz → admission gates a request actually passes through. |\n1030\t| Ch 13 / Ch 16 | The debugging commands riding the outbound paths — split per above. |\n1031\t| Ch 15 | **The same shape with a Git repository in the hub position.** Ch 3 names Argo CD and source-tags it here precisely so the recognition lands. |\n1032\t\n1033\t## Related\n1034\t\n1035\t[[control-loop]] · [[control-plane-components]] · [[orchestration-technical-definition]]\n1036\t=== END WRITE ===\n1037\t\n1038\t=== WRITE C:\\dev\\lodestar\\certcomp\\..\\Book-KCNA/knowledge-base/concepts/optional-components.md ===\n1039\t# Concept: What is optional, and whose word that is\n1040\t\n1041\t**Home:** Chapter 3 §4 · **Competency:** D1.1 · **Status:** canonical\n1042\t\n1043\t## The count\n1044\t\n1045\tEight components and four addons. **Exactly two of the twelve carry the word \"optional\" in\n1046\tthe documentation itself:** kube-proxy and cloud-controller-manager\n1047\t[source: k8s-docs-components-2026-08-23]. They carry it for **two genuinely different\n1048\treasons.** Addons are a third kind of optional, and Chapter 3 marks that framing as **its\n1049\town** — the docs say addons *extend* the cluster and never call them optional.\n1050\t\n1051\t| What | Why it's optional | Whose word |\n1052\t|---|---|---|\n1053\t| **kube-proxy** | Because something else can do its job — a network plugin implementing equivalent packet forwarding for Services. | The documentation's |\n1054\t| **cloud-controller-manager** | Because there may be no cloud to talk to. On premises or on a laptop, the cluster simply does not have one. | The documentation's |\n1055\t| **Addons** | Because they extend rather than constitute. The cluster is a cluster without them. | **This book's framing** |\n1056\t\n1057\tThat whose-word column is a Part 14 compliance feature, not decoration. Preserve it.\n1058\t\n1059\t## ⚠ Navigational Hazards (verbatim)\n1060\t\n1061\t> **\"kube-proxy runs on every node, always.\"** The documentation says node components run\n1062\t> on every node, then marks kube-proxy optional in the same breath. Both are true: *when it\n1063\t> runs, it runs on every node*, but it may not run at all.\n1064\t>\n1065\t> **\"Every cluster has a cloud-controller-manager.\"** No. Every cluster on a cloud provider\n1066\t> that integrates one does. On premises, or on a laptop, that component is simply absent.\n1067\t>\n1068\t> The shared lesson: **read the word \"optional\" when the documentation prints it.** It's\n1069\t> printed rarely and deliberately, on exactly two of the eight components.\n1070\t\n1071\t## The distinction worth preserving: absent vs present-but-idle\n1072\t\n1073\tBearings #2 Q2's most interesting distractor asserts cloud-controller-manager runs\n1074\teverywhere \"but on the laptop and in the data center it runs with no provider configured.\"\n1075\tChapter 3's rationale: \"The component isn't present-but-idle; it is **absent**. An\n1076\tidle-but-present component would still turn up in a component listing on that cluster. This\n1077\tone doesn't.\"\n1078\t\n1079\t## Addons\n1080\t\n1081\t\"Addons extend the functionality of Kubernetes.\" Published examples: **DNS**, **Web UI\n1082\t(Dashboard)**, **Container Resource Monitoring**, **Cluster-level Logging**\n1083\t[source: k8s-docs-components-2026-08-23] [source: k8s-docs-cluster-addons-2026-08-24 — ⚑\n1084\tNOT ON DISK]. \"Note the word *extend*. Addons are not components in the sense that the\n1085\teight names in §3 are components. They run *on* the cluster rather than constituting it.\"\n1086\t\n1087\t**Spelling: `addon`, one word.** Chapter 3 line ~466 reads \"add-on\" once — flagged.\n1088\t\n1089\t## Where this section leads\n1090\t\n1091\t§4's whole purpose is the ⚓ that closes it — see [[absent-component-pattern]]. The\n1092\toptionality material is the setup; the pattern is the payload.\n1093\t\n1094\t## Related\n1095\t\n1096\t[[absent-component-pattern]] · [[node-components]] · [[control-plane-components]]\n1097\t=== END WRITE ===\n1098\t\n1099\t=== WRITE C:\\dev\\lodestar\\certcomp\\..\\Book-KCNA/knowledge-base/concepts/orchestration-technical-definition.md ===\n1100\t# Concept: Orchestration — the technical definition Kubernetes disclaims\n1101\t\n1102\t**Home:** Chapter 3 §7 (planted §1) · **Competency:** D1.1 · **Status:** canonical\n1103\t**This is Chapter 3's ☀️ Zenith.**\n1104\t\n1105\t## ★ Fixed Point (verbatim)\n1106\t\n1107\t> **Orchestration, technically, is the execution of a defined workflow: first do A, then\n1108\t> B, then C.** Kubernetes disclaims it. Kubernetes comprises a set of independent,\n1109\t> composable control processes that continuously drive current state toward desired state\n1110\t> — and it shouldn't matter how you get from A to C. Centralized control is not required.\n1111\t> [source: k8s-docs-overview-2026-08-23]\n1112\t\n1113\t## The documentation's full passage\n1114\t\n1115\t> \"Kubernetes is not a mere orchestration system. In fact, it eliminates the need for\n1116\t> orchestration. **The technical definition of orchestration is execution of a defined\n1117\t> workflow: first do A, then B, then C.** In contrast, Kubernetes comprises a set of\n1118\t> independent, composable control processes that continuously drive the current state\n1119\t> towards the provided desired state. It shouldn't matter how you get from A to C.\n1120\t> Centralized control is also not required. This results in a system that is easier to\n1121\t> use and more powerful, robust, resilient, and extensible.\"\n1122\t\n1123\t## Two senses, both real — the book uses both deliberately\n1124\t\n1125\t| Sense | Meaning | Where |\n1126\t|---|---|---|\n1127\t| **Loose (industry)** | \"a thing that manages containers across machines\" | Ch 1 Soundings A2: \"Kubernetes is an **orchestrator** — it decides what should run where.\" |\n1128\t| **Precise (technical)** | \"execution of a defined workflow: A then B then C\" | Ch 3 §7 — **the one thing Kubernetes says it is not** |\n1129\t\n1130\tChapter 3 §1's 🪝 Snag plants the tension and names it as **the book correcting its own\n1131\tearlier wording, not the reader**: \"At orientation altitude, that's the right answer to the\n1132\tquestion being asked… But it's the loose sense of the word. The precise sense is the one on\n1133\tthe exam.\"\n1134\t\n1135\t**Glossary consequence:** these are **two entries, not one.** See the Rule 6 note in\n1136\t`glossary.md` under *orchestrator (loose sense)*.\n1137\t\n1138\t## The synthesis §7 performs\n1139\t\n1140\t§5 (all state through one hub; coordination is watching, not telling) + §6 (many\n1141\tindependent non-terminating loops) ⇒ **there is no component in charge, and now you can see\n1142\twhy there isn't one.** \"There's a description of what should be true, and a set of\n1143\tindependent processes each responsible for one gap between description and reality. Nobody\n1144\tis executing steps, because nobody wrote steps. What looked like a missing piece of the\n1145\tarchitecture *is* the architecture.\"\n1146\t\n1147\t## ⚠ The narrowing — preserve it, it is a Part 14 exemplar\n1148\t\n1149\tChapter 3 disarms its own chapter title:\n1150\t\n1151\t> \"'Nobody is in charge' is a good chapter title and a bad thesis… The control plane *does*\n1152\t> make global decisions. The API server *is* a hub that everything depends on. etcd *is* a\n1153\t> single store whose loss loses the cluster… The accurate claim is narrower and better:\n1154\t> **there is no component that executes a workflow, and no component that tells another\n1155\t> component what to do.** That's a claim about *coordination*, not about *availability*.\"\n1156\t\n1157\t**Chapters 8, 12, and 13 depend on the narrower version.** Do not let the slogan travel\n1158\twithout it.\n1159\t\n1160\t## One claim that was deliberately reframed — do not restore\n1161\t\n1162\tAn earlier draft asserted specific kubelet behavior during a control-plane partition (\"it\n1163\tkeeps the containers it already knows about running\"). **No cached or fetched snapshot\n1164\tcovers kubelet behavior when the API server is unreachable** — `k8s-docs-nodes-2026-08-23`\n1165\tdocuments only the control-plane side (Ready → Unknown, API-initiated eviction). The claim\n1166\tis now framed as a consequence of the sourced \"ensures the containers described in those\n1167\tPodSpecs are running\" description. To state partition behavior explicitly, open a research\n1168\tgap for the kubelet reference first.\n1169\t\n1170\t## Downstream obligations\n1171\t\n1172\t| Chapter | Obligation |\n1173\t|---|---|\n1174\t| Ch 15 | Primary Zenith. GitOps as the same shape pointed at a Git repository. |\n1175\t| Ch 17 | \"The same pattern, named as a principle\" — declarative APIs in the cloud native definition. |\n1176\t\n1177\t## Related\n1178\t\n1179\t[[control-loop]] · [[api-server-hub]] · [[what-kubernetes-is-not]]\n1180\t=== END WRITE ===\n1181\t\n1182\t=== WRITE C:\\dev\\lodestar\\certcomp\\..\\Book-KCNA/knowledge-base/concepts/deployment-eras.md ===\n1183\t# Concept: The three deployment eras\n1184\t\n1185\t**Home:** Chapter 3 §1 · **Competency:** D1.1 · **Status:** canonical\n1186\t**Prerequisite:** Chapter 2 §1 (the container/VM architectural contrast, in detail)\n1187\t\n1188\t## The three eras (documentation wording)\n1189\t\n1190\t**Traditional.** \"Organizations ran applications on physical servers. There was no way to\n1191\tdefine resource boundaries for applications on a physical server, and this caused\n1192\tresource-allocation issues… The available solution was to run each application on a\n1193\tdifferent physical server, which did not scale.\"\n1194\t\n1195\t**Virtualized.** \"Virtualization allowed multiple Virtual Machines to run on a single\n1196\tphysical server's CPU. Applications became isolated between VMs… But each VM is a full\n1197\tmachine running all the components, including its own operating system, on top of\n1198\tvirtualized hardware.\"\n1199\t\n1200\t**Container.** \"Containers are similar to VMs, but they have relaxed isolation properties\n1201\tthat let them share the operating system among the applications. That's why containers are\n1202\tconsidered lightweight. Like a VM, a container has its own filesystem, share of CPU,\n1203\tmemory, process space, and more. Because they are decoupled from the underlying\n1204\tinfrastructure, they are portable across clouds and OS distributions.\"\n1205\t[all: source: k8s-docs-overview-2026-08-23]\n1206\t\n1207\t## The consequence — which is why §1 exists\n1208\t\n1209\tChapter 3 explicitly does **not** re-teach the container/VM architecture (Chapter 2 did).\n1210\tWhat §1 adds: \"Once your unit of deployment is small, fast, and portable, you stop having a\n1211\thandful of servers with one application each and start having hundreds of interchangeable\n1212\tprocesses scattered across a pool of machines. That is a fundamentally different management\n1213\tproblem, and it's the problem Kubernetes was built for.\"\n1214\t\n1215\t## Figure\n1216\t\n1217\t`ch03-fig03-deployment-eras-timeline`. Caption: \"What changes across the eras is not the\n1218\tapplication. It's what the application shares with everything else on the machine. Read the\n1219\tbottom row.\" The bottom row reads: shares nothing → shares hardware → shares the operating\n1220\tsystem.\n1221\t\n1222\t## ⚑ RULE 6 — the kernel/OS register, and a note that misreports it\n1223\t\n1224\t**The substance is settled and all three chapters agree.** Chapter 2 §1 (ch02:281–285)\n1225\tadjudicates both registers as correct and instructs the reader to hold both:\n1226\t\n1227\t> \"The Kubernetes documentation and the CNCF glossary say a container **shares the\n1228\t> operating system**. That is the phrasing the exam is likeliest to use, and the one to\n1229\t> recognize on an answer sheet. Practitioners and the container-runtime documentation\n1230\t> usually sharpen it: a container **shares the host's kernel**… Hold both registers:\n1231\t> **operating system** as the published wording, **kernel** as the mechanism underneath it.\"\n1232\t\n1233\tChapter 2 supplies an in-cache warrant for the sharpening — Kubernetes describes a\n1234\tsandboxed runtime as supplying \"a user-space kernel (such as gVisor)\"\n1235\t[source: k8s-docs-runtime-class-2026-08-23] — a phrase only meaningful if the ordinary case\n1236\tis the *host's* kernel.\n1237\t\n1238\t**Chapter 3 follows Chapter 2 correctly.** It quotes the snapshot when citing, then carries\n1239\tthe kernel precision as a visibly authorial, untagged sentence: *\"The sharper statement,\n1240\tthat what a container shares with the host is specifically the **kernel**, is this book's,\n1241\tnot the documentation's.\"*\n1242\t\n1243\t**⚠ Chapter 3's §1 AUTHOR-REVIEW (draft line ~130) misreports the state of the book.** It\n1244\tcites ch02:279 alone (which matches the snapshot) rather than ch02:281–285 (which\n1245\tadjudicates), and concludes \"the remaining outlier is Chapter 1.\"\n1246\t\n1247\t**Chapter 1 is not an outlier in content.** ch01:142 uses the kernel register that Chapter 2\n1248\texplicitly blesses. The only live question is **attribution**: whether that bolded phrase\n1249\tmay sit on a line tagged `k8s-docs-overview-2026-08-23`. Blocked on harvest items A13/A14,\n1250\tper Chapter 2's own AUTHOR-REVIEW, which prescribes deleting Chapter 1's parallel flag once\n1251\tthey land.\n1252\t\n1253\t**Risk if acted on as written:** an author would flatten ch01:142 toward \"operating\n1254\tsystem,\" deleting the register Chapter 2 spends three paragraphs establishing and that\n1255\tFigure 2-1's derivation (\"Three guest kernels on one host means three times the baseline\n1256\tresource cost\") depends on. **Do not act on the note. Rewrite it first.**\n1257\t\n1258\tChapter 3's body prose needs no change.\n1259\t\n1260\t## Retrieval note\n1261\t\n1262\tPractice Q2 is tagged `[retrieval: ch2]` and the tag is honest — Chapter 2 does teach it.\n1263\tBut **the answer is restated verbatim in this chapter's own §1**, ~70 minutes of reading\n1264\tearlier, so the item functions as same-chapter recall rather than *spaced* retrieval. See\n1265\t`retrieval-log.md` for options. Its answer key should also carry Chapter 2's one-clause\n1266\ttwo-register note, so a reader holding Ch 1's \"kernel\" answer isn't silently contradicted.\n1267\t\n1268\t## Related\n1269\t\n1270\t[[what-kubernetes-is-not]] · [[kubernetes-origin]] · [[node-components]]\n1271\t=== END WRITE ===\n1272\t\n1273\t=== WRITE C:\\dev\\lodestar\\certcomp\\..\\Book-KCNA/knowledge-base/concepts/what-kubernetes-is-not.md ===\n1274\t# Concept: What Kubernetes is (and explicitly is not)\n1275\t\n1276\t**Home:** Chapter 3 §1 · **Competency:** D1.1 · **Status:** canonical\n1277\t**Exam Alert priority #5.** The negative list is tested more than its length suggests.\n1278\t\n1279\t## Not a traditional PaaS\n1280\t\n1281\t\"Kubernetes is not a traditional, all-inclusive PaaS. Because it operates at the container\n1282\tlevel rather than the hardware level, it provides some features common to PaaS offerings\n1283\t(deployment, scaling, load balancing) and lets users integrate their own logging,\n1284\tmonitoring, and alerting. But Kubernetes is not monolithic, and those default solutions are\n1285\toptional and pluggable. It provides the building blocks for building developer platforms\n1286\twhile preserving user choice.\" [source: k8s-docs-overview-2026-08-23]\n1287\t\n1288\t## The six explicit disclaimers (verbatim, all one source)\n1289\t\n1290\tKubernetes:\n1291\t- **Does not limit the types of applications supported.** \"If an application can run in a\n1292\t  container, it should run great on Kubernetes.\"\n1293\t- **Does not deploy source code and does not build your application.** \"CI/CD workflows are\n1294\t  determined by organizational culture and preferences as well as technical requirements.\"\n1295\t- **Does not provide application-level services** — no middleware (message buses), no\n1296\t  data-processing frameworks (Spark), no databases (MySQL), no caches, no cluster storage\n1297\t  systems (Ceph) as built-in services.\n1298\t- **Does not dictate logging, monitoring, or alerting solutions.**\n1299\t- **Does not provide nor mandate a configuration language or system.** \"It provides a\n1300\t  declarative API that arbitrary declarative specifications may target.\"\n1301\t- **Does not provide nor adopt any comprehensive machine configuration, maintenance,\n1302\t  management, or self-healing systems.**\n1303\t\n1304\tPlus the seventh, held over to §7: **\"not a mere orchestration system.\"**\n1305\t\n1306\t## What Kubernetes provides — and the re-read that matters\n1307\t\n1308\tThe published capability list: service discovery and load balancing · storage orchestration\n1309\t· automated rollouts and rollbacks · automatic bin packing · self-healing · secret and\n1310\tconfiguration management · batch execution · horizontal scaling · IPv4/IPv6 dual-stack ·\n1311\tdesigned for extensibility.\n1312\t\n1313\tChapter 3's move is to read the list *as container-era problems*, then plant §6:\n1314\t\n1315\t> \"Read that right-hand column again. Notice how many entries describe something that has\n1316\t> to *keep being true* rather than something that has to *happen once*. Self-healing isn't\n1317\t> an action; it's a standing condition.\"\n1318\t\n1319\t§6 cashes it: self-healing \"stops being a feature and becomes a description of loops\n1320\trunning.\" Practice Q18 is built on exactly that re-read.\n1321\t\n1322\t## ⚑ Terminology gaps\n1323\t\n1324\t**PaaS** and **CI/CD** are both first-use-in-the-book here and **neither acronym is\n1325\texpanded.** PaaS has no owner chapter anywhere in B2. Either expand on first use or assign\n1326\tan owner.\n1327\t\n1328\t## Related\n1329\t\n1330\t[[orchestration-technical-definition]] · [[control-loop]] · [[deployment-eras]]\n1331\t=== END WRITE ==="}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b40d53a8-4207-4eb0-89d6-b74f450c421c","uuid":"021d04bf-3852-456e-9b12-f4103a596492","timestamp":"2026-08-24T09:52:36.307Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\.pipeline-state\\ch-03\\kb-manifest.md","content":"runtime environment\" [source: k8s-docs-components-2026-08-23]. Chapter 3 flags the verb:\nthe node's collective job is to **maintain** running Pods, \"not to start them once and\nstand down.\"\n\n## The three\n\n**kubelet** — \"An agent that runs on each node in the cluster. It makes sure that\ncontainers are running in a Pod. The kubelet takes a set of PodSpecs that are provided\nthrough various mechanisms and ensures that the containers described in those PodSpecs are\nrunning and healthy. **The kubelet doesn't manage containers which were not created by\nKubernetes.**\"\n\n**kube-proxy (optional)** — \"a network proxy that runs on each node in your cluster,\nimplementing **part** of the Kubernetes Service concept. kube-proxy maintains network rules\non nodes.\" Optional \"if you use a network plugin that implements packet forwarding for\nServices by itself, and providing equivalent behavior to kube-proxy.\"\n\n**container runtime** — \"A fundamental component that empowers Kubernetes to run containers\neffectively. It is responsible for managing the execution and lifecycle of containers\nwithin the Kubernetes environment.\" containerd, CRI-O, or any CRI implementation.\n\n## The clause with practical teeth\n\n\"If you SSH to a node and start a container by hand with the container runtime directly,\nthe kubelet does not manage it. It won't restart it, won't maintain it, won't clean it up.\"\nChapter 3 draws the two-way consequence: it is why hand-started containers don't appear\nthrough `kubectl`, **and** why node-level debugging needs a node-level tool (`crictl`,\nCh 13 §5 — a pointer Chapter 2 already made identically at ch02:602).\n\n## What §3 adds beyond Chapter 2: position\n\nThe container runtime \"is the one node component that is not Kubernetes software at all.\nIt's a separate project, swappable, and not authored by the Kubernetes project: Kubernetes\ndefines the interface and accepts any implementation of it.\"\n\n## ⚑ RULE 6 — this section broadens Chapter 2's named pattern\n\nChapter 3 §3 continues: *\"That is the same architectural instinct you saw with etcd. Where\na good general-purpose component already exists, Kubernetes defines an interface and uses\nit rather than reimplementing it.\"* **Two problems.**\n\n**(a) etcd is not in the enumeration.** Chapter 2's pattern is *\"Kubernetes defines an\ninterface and lets the **ecosystem** implement it\"* (ch02:598), and its sourced list is\n**CRI, CSI, CNI, device plugins, and API extensions**\n[source: k8s-docs-extending-kubernetes-2026-08-23]. Kubernetes does not define an etcd\ninterface; it consumes a general-purpose datastore. True of the runtime, true-but-different\nof etcd — calling them \"the same\" broadens a sourced list past what it says.\n\n**(b) The name is dropped.** ch02:596–598 is a section titled \"The pattern to name now\"\nwhose ⚓ tells the reader *\"Give this move a name in your head, because you are about to see\nit three more times.\"* Chapter 3 hits the **first recurrence**, re-derives it in fresh\nwords, and never uses the name or bears back. That is a missed spaced-retrieval event on a\ntheme B3 tracks and Ch 17's secondary Zenith depends on.\n\n**Fix:** keep the named pattern for the container runtime, cross-beared to Ch 2 §4;\ndescribe etcd as the adjacent-but-distinct instinct of reusing an existing general-purpose\ncomponent.\n\n## ★ Fixed Point — the full census (verbatim)\n\n> **Control plane:** kube-apiserver, etcd, kube-scheduler, kube-controller-manager, and\n> cloud-controller-manager *(optional — absent on premises and on your laptop)*.\n>\n> **Node** (on every node): kubelet, kube-proxy *(optional — unnecessary if a network\n> plugin provides equivalent packet forwarding)*, and the container runtime.\n>\n> Eight names. Two of them optional, for two different reasons. One of them — the\n> container runtime — is not Kubernetes software.\n\n## Related\n\n[[control-plane-components]] · [[optional-components]] · [[api-server-hub]]\n=== END WRITE ===\n\n=== WRITE C:\\dev\\lodestar\\certcomp\\..\\Book-KCNA/knowledge-base/concepts/api-server-hub.md ===\n# Concept: The API server as hub — \"watching, not telling\"\n\n**Home:** Chapter 3 §5 · **Competency:** D1.1 · **Status:** canonical\n**Chapter 3's second-most-important idea**, after the control loop.\n\n## The four facts that compose into a shape\n\n1. \"The API server is the front end for the Kubernetes control plane.\"\n   [source: k8s-docs-cluster-architecture-2026-08-23]\n2. etcd is \"the backing store for all cluster data.\" [same]\n3. \"Kubernetes has a hub-and-spoke API pattern. All API usage from nodes and the Pods they\n   run terminates at the API server, and none of the other control-plane components are\n   designed to expose remote services.\"\n   [source: k8s-docs-control-plane-node-communication-2026-08-24 — ⚑ NOT ON DISK]\n4. A controller \"might carry the action out itself; more commonly, in Kubernetes, a\n   controller will send messages to the API server that have useful side effects.\"\n   [source: k8s-docs-controllers-2026-08-23]\n\nChapter 3's image for the result: **\"Not a chain of command. A chart table: one surface\neveryone works from, and no orders issued across it.\"**\n\n## ⚓ The reusable sentence (verbatim)\n\n> **The coordination mechanism in Kubernetes is watching, not telling.** Components don't\n> send each other instructions. They observe shared state and act on what they see.\n\n## Two precisions the chapter is careful about — preserve both\n\n**1. etcd confinement is a RECOMMENDATION, not an invariant.** \"Access to etcd is\nequivalent to root permission in the cluster, so **ideally** only the API server should\nhave access to it\" [source: k8s-docs-etcd-access-control-2026-08-24 — ⚑ NOT ON DISK].\nChapter 3 states this explicitly: \"Read that as what it is: a strong recommendation with a\nsecurity reason behind it, not a law of physics.\" **Do not harden it downstream.**\n\n**2. The hub describes STATE movement, not every connection.** \"The API server does open\nconnections to kubelets: that is how fetching Pod logs, attaching to a running container,\nand port-forwarding work. Those paths carry a session, not an instruction. Nothing\ntravelling on them tells a kubelet what ought to be running.\"\n\nAn earlier draft asserted the absolute form; a prior AUTHOR-REVIEW records that it was\nsoftened deliberately after the source was fetched. **Do not restore the absolute\nphrasing.**\n\n## The submission story (component altitude)\n\n1. Request arrives at the API server; validated and persisted. **Nothing is running yet.**\n2. The description is now part of cluster state, visible to anything watching.\n3. The scheduler notices a Pod with no node, selects one, records the selection back\n   through the API server.\n4. The kubelet on that node notices and starts the containers through the runtime.\n5. Status flows back the same way.\n\n\"Read the verbs. *Notices. Selects. Records. Notices.* At no point does one component\ninstruct another.\"\n\n## Figure\n\n`ch03-fig04-request-path-through-apiserver`. The caption instructs the reader to look for\nthe arrow that **isn't** drawn: there is none between any two of the surrounding\ncomponents. The figure is scoped to the **state/API path** and deliberately does not draw\nthe outbound logs/attach/port-forward paths.\n\n## ⚑ Forward-bearing split needed\n\nCh 3 §5 bears the outbound paths to \"Ch 13 — the debugging commands that ride those\noutbound paths.\" B2 gives `logs --previous` to Ch 13 but **`exec` and `port-forward` to\nCh 16.** Split the bearing.\n\n## Downstream obligations\n\n| Chapter | Obligation |\n|---|---|\n| Ch 8 | The auth → authz → admission gates a request actually passes through. |\n| Ch 13 / Ch 16 | The debugging commands riding the outbound paths — split per above. |\n| Ch 15 | **The same shape with a Git repository in the hub position.** Ch 3 names Argo CD and source-tags it here precisely so the recognition lands. |\n\n## Related\n\n[[control-loop]] · [[control-plane-components]] · [[orchestration-technical-definition]]\n=== END WRITE ===\n\n=== WRITE C:\\dev\\lodestar\\certcomp\\..\\Book-KCNA/knowledge-base/concepts/optional-components.md ===\n# Concept: What is optional, and whose word that is\n\n**Home:** Chapter 3 §4 · **Competency:** D1.1 · **Status:** canonical\n\n## The count\n\nEight components and four addons. **Exactly two of the twelve carry the word \"optional\" in\nthe documentation itself:** kube-proxy and cloud-controller-manager\n[source: k8s-docs-components-2026-08-23]. They carry it for **two genuinely different\nreasons.** Addons are a third kind of optional, and Chapter 3 marks that framing as **its\nown** — the docs say addons *extend* the cluster and never call them optional.\n\n| What | Why it's optional | Whose word |\n|---|---|---|\n| **kube-proxy** | Because something else can do its job — a network plugin implementing equivalent packet forwarding for Services. | The documentation's |\n| **cloud-controller-manager** | Because there may be no cloud to talk to. On premises or on a laptop, the cluster simply does not have one. | The documentation's |\n| **Addons** | Because they extend rather than constitute. The cluster is a cluster without them. | **This book's framing** |\n\nThat whose-word column is a Part 14 compliance feature, not decoration. Preserve it.\n\n## ⚠ Navigational Hazards (verbatim)\n\n> **\"kube-proxy runs on every node, always.\"** The documentation says node components run\n> on every node, then marks kube-proxy optional in the same breath. Both are true: *when it\n> runs, it runs on every node*, but it may not run at all.\n>\n> **\"Every cluster has a cloud-controller-manager.\"** No. Every cluster on a cloud provider\n> that integrates one does. On premises, or on a laptop, that component is simply absent.\n>\n> The shared lesson: **read the word \"optional\" when the documentation prints it.** It's\n> printed rarely and deliberately, on exactly two of the eight components.\n\n## The distinction worth preserving: absent vs present-but-idle\n\nBearings #2 Q2's most interesting distractor asserts cloud-controller-manager runs\neverywhere \"but on the laptop and in the data center it runs with no provider configured.\"\nChapter 3's rationale: \"The component isn't present-but-idle; it is **absent**. An\nidle-but-present component would still turn up in a component listing on that cluster. This\none doesn't.\"\n\n## Addons\n\n\"Addons extend the functionality of Kubernetes.\" Published examples: **DNS**, **Web UI\n(Dashboard)**, **Container Resource Monitoring**, **Cluster-level Logging**\n[source: k8s-docs-components-2026-08-23] [source: k8s-docs-cluster-addons-2026-08-24 — ⚑\nNOT ON DISK]. \"Note the word *extend*. Addons are not components in the sense that the\neight names in §3 are components. They run *on* the cluster rather than constituting it.\"\n\n**Spelling: `addon`, one word.** Chapter 3 line ~466 reads \"add-on\" once — flagged.\n\n## Where this section leads\n\n§4's whole purpose is the ⚓ that closes it — see [[absent-component-pattern]]. The\noptionality material is the setup; the pattern is the payload.\n\n## Related\n\n[[absent-component-pattern]] · [[node-components]] · [[control-plane-components]]\n=== END WRITE ===\n\n=== WRITE C:\\dev\\lodestar\\certcomp\\..\\Book-KCNA/knowledge-base/concepts/orchestration-technical-definition.md ===\n# Concept: Orchestration — the technical definition Kubernetes disclaims\n\n**Home:** Chapter 3 §7 (planted §1) · **Competency:** D1.1 · **Status:** canonical\n**This is Chapter 3's ☀️ Zenith.**\n\n## ★ Fixed Point (verbatim)\n\n> **Orchestration, technically, is the execution of a defined workflow: first do A, then\n> B, then C.** Kubernetes disclaims it. Kubernetes comprises a set of independent,\n> composable control processes that continuously drive current state toward desired state\n> — and it shouldn't matter how you get from A to C. Centralized control is not required.\n> [source: k8s-docs-overview-2026-08-23]\n\n## The documentation's full passage\n\n> \"Kubernetes is not a mere orchestration system. In fact, it eliminates the need for\n> orchestration. **The technical definition of orchestration is execution of a defined\n> workflow: first do A, then B, then C.** In contrast, Kubernetes comprises a set of\n> independent, composable control processes that continuously drive the current state\n> towards the provided desired state. It shouldn't matter how you get from A to C.\n> Centralized control is also not required. This results in a system that is easier to\n> use and more powerful, robust, resilient, and extensible.\"\n\n## Two senses, both real — the book uses both deliberately\n\n| Sense | Meaning | Where |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Loose (industry)** | \"a thing that manages containers across machines\" | Ch 1 Soundings A2: \"Kubernetes is an **orchestrator** — it decides what should run where.\" |\n| **Precise (technical)** | \"execution of a defined workflow: A then B then C\" | Ch 3 §7 — **the one thing Kubernetes says it is not** |\n\nChapter 3 §1's 🪝 Snag plants the tension and names it as **the book correcting its own\nearlier wording, not the reader**: \"At orientation altitude, that's the right answer to the\nquestion being asked… But it's the loose sense of the word. The precise sense is the one on\nthe exam.\"\n\n**Glossary consequence:** these are **two entries, not one.** See the Rule 6 note in\n`glossary.md` under *orchestrator (loose sense)*.\n\n## The synthesis §7 performs\n\n§5 (all state through one hub; coordination is watching, not telling) + §6 (many\nindependent non-terminating loops) ⇒ **there is no component in charge, and now you can see\nwhy there isn't one.** \"There's a description of what should be true, and a set of\nindependent processes each responsible for one gap between description and reality. Nobody\nis executing steps, because nobody wrote steps. What looked like a missing piece of the\narchitecture *is* the architecture.\"\n\n## ⚠ The narrowing — preserve it, it is a Part 14 exemplar\n\nChapter 3 disarms its own chapter title:\n\n> \"'Nobody is in charge' is a good chapter title and a bad thesis… The control plane *does*\n> make global decisions. The API server *is* a hub that everything depends on. etcd *is* a\n> single store whose loss loses the cluster… The accurate claim is narrower and better:\n> **there is no component that executes a workflow, and no component that tells another\n> component what to do.** That's a claim about *coordination*, not about *availability*.\"\n\n**Chapters 8, 12, and 13 depend on the narrower version.** Do not let the slogan travel\nwithout it.\n\n## One claim that was deliberately reframed — do not restore\n\nAn earlier draft asserted specific kubelet behavior during a control-plane partition (\"it\nkeeps the containers it already knows about running\"). **No cached or fetched snapshot\ncovers kubelet behavior when the API server is unreachable** — `k8s-docs-nodes-2026-08-23`\ndocuments only the control-plane side (Ready → Unknown, API-initiated eviction). The claim\nis now framed as a consequence of the sourced \"ensures the containers described in those\nPodSpecs are running\" description. To state partition behavior explicitly, open a research\ngap for the kubelet reference first.\n\n## Downstream obligations\n\n| Chapter | Obligation |\n|---|---|\n| Ch 15 | Primary Zenith. GitOps as the same shape pointed at a Git repository. |\n| Ch 17 | \"The same pattern, named as a principle\" — declarative APIs in the cloud native definition. |\n\n## Related\n\n[[control-loop]] · [[api-server-hub]] · [[what-kubernetes-is-not]]\n=== END WRITE ===\n\n=== WRITE C:\\dev\\lodestar\\certcomp\\..\\Book-KCNA/knowledge-base/concepts/deployment-eras.md ===\n# Concept: The three deployment eras\n\n**Home:** Chapter 3 §1 · **Competency:** D1.1 · **Status:** canonical\n**Prerequisite:** Chapter 2 §1 (the container/VM architectural contrast, in detail)\n\n## The three eras (documentation wording)\n\n**Traditional.** \"Organizations ran applications on physical servers. There was no way to\ndefine resource boundaries for applications on a physical server, and this caused\nresource-allocation issues… The available solution was to run each application on a\ndifferent physical server, which did not scale.\"\n\n**Virtualized.** \"Virtualization allowed multiple Virtual Machines to run on a single\nphysical server's CPU. Applications became isolated between VMs… But each VM is a full\nmachine running all the components, including its own operating system, on top of\nvirtualized hardware.\"\n\n**Container.** \"Containers are similar to VMs, but they have relaxed isolation properties\nthat let them share the operating system among the applications. That's why containers are\nconsidered lightweight. Like a VM, a container has its own filesystem, share of CPU,\nmemory, process space, and more. Because they are decoupled from the underlying\ninfrastructure, they are portable across clouds and OS distributions.\"\n[all: source: k8s-docs-overview-2026-08-23]\n\n## The consequence — which is why §1 exists\n\nChapter 3 explicitly does **not** re-teach the container/VM architecture (Chapter 2 did).\nWhat §1 adds: \"Once your unit of deployment is small, fast, and portable, you stop having a\nhandful of servers with one application each and start having hundreds of interchangeable\nprocesses scattered across a pool of machines. That is a fundamentally different management\nproblem, and it's the problem Kubernetes was built for.\"\n\n## Figure\n\n`ch03-fig03-deployment-eras-timeline`. Caption: \"What changes across the eras is not the\napplication. It's what the application shares with everything else on the machine. Read the\nbottom row.\" The bottom row reads: shares nothing → shares hardware → shares the operating\nsystem.\n\n## ⚑ RULE 6 — the kernel/OS register, and a note that misreports it\n\n**The substance is settled and all three chapters agree.** Chapter 2 §1 (ch02:281–285)\nadjudicates both registers as correct and instructs the reader to hold both:\n\n> \"The Kubernetes documentation and the CNCF glossary say a container **shares the\n> operating system**. That is the phrasing the exam is likeliest to use, and the one to\n> recognize on an answer sheet. Practitioners and the container-runtime documentation\n> usually sharpen it: a container **shares the host's kernel**… Hold both registers:\n> **operating system** as the published wording, **kernel** as the mechanism underneath it.\"\n\nChapter 2 supplies an in-cache warrant for the sharpening — Kubernetes describes a\nsandboxed runtime as supplying \"a user-space kernel (such as gVisor)\"\n[source: k8s-docs-runtime-class-2026-08-23] — a phrase only meaningful if the ordinary case\nis the *host's* kernel.\n\n**Chapter 3 follows Chapter 2 correctly.** It quotes the snapshot when citing, then carries\nthe kernel precision as a visibly authorial, untagged sentence: *\"The sharper statement,\nthat what a container shares with the host is specifically the **kernel**, is this book's,\nnot the documentation's.\"*\n\n**⚠ Chapter 3's §1 AUTHOR-REVIEW (draft line ~130) misreports the state of the book.** It\ncites ch02:279 alone (which matches the snapshot) rather than ch02:281–285 (which\nadjudicates), and concludes \"the remaining outlier is Chapter 1.\"\n\n**Chapter 1 is not an outlier in content.** ch01:142 uses the kernel register that Chapter 2\nexplicitly blesses. The only live question is **attribution**: whether that bolded phrase\nmay sit on a line tagged `k8s-docs-overview-2026-08-23`. Blocked on harvest items A13/A14,\nper Chapter 2's own AUTHOR-REVIEW, which prescribes deleting Chapter 1's parallel flag once\nthey land.\n\n**Risk if acted on as written:** an author would flatten ch01:142 toward \"operating\nsystem,\" deleting the register Chapter 2 spends three paragraphs establishing and that\nFigure 2-1's derivation (\"Three guest kernels on one host means three times the baseline\nresource cost\") depends on. **Do not act on the note. Rewrite it first.**\n\nChapter 3's body prose needs no change.\n\n## Retrieval note\n\nPractice Q2 is tagged `[retrieval: ch2]` and the tag is honest — Chapter 2 does teach it.\nBut **the answer is restated verbatim in this chapter's own §1**, ~70 minutes of reading\nearlier, so the item functions as same-chapter recall rather than *spaced* retrieval. See\n`retrieval-log.md` for options. Its answer key should also carry Chapter 2's one-clause\ntwo-register note, so a reader holding Ch 1's \"kernel\" answer isn't silently contradicted.\n\n## Related\n\n[[what-kubernetes-is-not]] · [[kubernetes-origin]] · [[node-components]]\n=== END WRITE ===\n\n=== WRITE C:\\dev\\lodestar\\certcomp\\..\\Book-KCNA/knowledge-base/concepts/what-kubernetes-is-not.md ===\n# Concept: What Kubernetes is (and explicitly is not)\n\n**Home:** Chapter 3 §1 · **Competency:** D1.1 · **Status:** canonical\n**Exam Alert priority #5.** The negative list is tested more than its length suggests.\n\n## Not a traditional PaaS\n\n\"Kubernetes is not a traditional, all-inclusive PaaS. Because it operates at the container\nlevel rather than the hardware level, it provides some features common to PaaS offerings\n(deployment, scaling, load balancing) and lets users integrate their own logging,\nmonitoring, and alerting. But Kubernetes is not monolithic, and those default solutions are\noptional and pluggable. It provides the building blocks for building developer platforms\nwhile preserving user choice.\" [source: k8s-docs-overview-2026-08-23]\n\n## The six explicit disclaimers (verbatim, all one source)\n\nKubernetes:\n- **Does not limit the types of applications supported.** \"If an application can run in a\n  container, it should run great on Kubernetes.\"\n- **Does not deploy source code and does not build your application.** \"CI/CD workflows are\n  determined by organizational culture and preferences as well as technical requirements.\"\n- **Does not provide application-level services** — no middleware (message buses), no\n  data-processing frameworks (Spark), no databases (MySQL), no caches, no cluster storage\n  systems (Ceph) as built-in services.\n- **Does not dictate logging, monitoring, or alerting solutions.**\n- **Does not provide nor mandate a configuration language or system.** \"It provides a\n  declarative API that arbitrary declarative specifications may target.\"\n- **Does not provide nor adopt any comprehensive machine configuration, maintenance,\n  management, or self-healing systems.**\n\nPlus the seventh, held over to §7: **\"not a mere orchestration system.\"**\n\n## What Kubernetes provides — and the re-read that matters\n\nThe published capability list: service discovery and load balancing · storage orchestration\n· automated rollouts and rollbacks · automatic bin packing · self-healing · secret and\nconfiguration management · batch execution · horizontal scaling · IPv4/IPv6 dual-stack ·\ndesigned for extensibility.\n\nChapter 3's move is to read the list *as container-era problems*, then plant §6:\n\n> \"Read that right-hand column again. Notice how many entries describe something that has\n> to *keep being true* rather than something that has to *happen once*. Self-healing isn't\n> an action; it's a standing condition.\"\n\n§6 cashes it: self-healing \"stops being a feature and becomes a description of loops\nrunning.\" Practice Q18 is built on exactly that re-read.\n\n## ⚑ Terminology gaps\n\n**PaaS** and **CI/CD** are both first-use-in-the-book here and **neither acronym is\nexpanded.** PaaS has no owner chapter anywhere in B2. Either expand on first use or assign\nan owner.\n\n## Related\n\n[[orchestration-technical-definition]] · [[control-loop]] · [[deployment-eras]]\n=== END WRITE ===","numLines":450,"startLine":882,"totalLines":1696}}}
